WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.192 --> 00:00:00.900
JOANNA SMITH: Hi.

00:00:00.900 --> 00:00:02.160
I'm Joanna Smith,
and today we'll

00:00:02.160 --> 00:00:04.160
look at how Stream Analytics
from a Google Cloud

00:00:04.160 --> 00:00:08.100
helps bring the real-time
e-commerce experience to life.

00:00:08.100 --> 00:00:09.570
Real-time data is
unique in that it

00:00:09.570 --> 00:00:11.400
provides an opportunity
for businesses

00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:13.380
to react without delay.

00:00:13.380 --> 00:00:15.850
Valuable events, such as
an online shopper clicking

00:00:15.850 --> 00:00:17.580
on an item's
details, can trigger

00:00:17.580 --> 00:00:19.560
dynamic and
immediate experiences

00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:21.540
on a website or mobile app.

00:00:21.540 --> 00:00:23.880
The opportunity to generate
value around this data type

00:00:23.880 --> 00:00:25.090
is only growing.

00:00:25.090 --> 00:00:27.060
In fact, the International
Data Corporation

00:00:27.060 --> 00:00:29.370
estimates that within five
years, more than a quarter

00:00:29.370 --> 00:00:32.700
of all data globally will
be real-time in nature.

00:00:32.700 --> 00:00:35.250
But for an application to
react to real-time data,

00:00:35.250 --> 00:00:37.590
it's crucial to have
data analysis, insights,

00:00:37.590 --> 00:00:40.830
and actions that occur
without human intervention.

00:00:40.830 --> 00:00:43.770
Stream Analytics does just
that, offering automated

00:00:43.770 --> 00:00:45.790
real-time data analysis.

00:00:45.790 --> 00:00:47.090
Let's take a look.

00:00:47.090 --> 00:00:49.157
Here, we've got our
e-commerce site, Newkicks.

00:00:49.157 --> 00:00:50.740
And on the right
throughout this demo,

00:00:50.740 --> 00:00:52.115
you'll see some
context on what's

00:00:52.115 --> 00:00:55.172
happening behind the
JavaScript on the GCP platform.

00:00:55.172 --> 00:00:56.880
Let's take a look at
how Stream Analytics

00:00:56.880 --> 00:00:58.830
can improve the Newkicks
shopping experience

00:00:58.830 --> 00:01:00.330
and drive sales.

00:01:00.330 --> 00:01:03.660
We've used Pub/Sub, Dataflow,
BigQuery and Bigtable

00:01:03.660 --> 00:01:05.820
to create the data streams
that will allow Newkicks

00:01:05.820 --> 00:01:08.160
to make their data actionable
within milliseconds

00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:09.840
of being generated.

00:01:09.840 --> 00:01:12.960
For example, the hot item
flag is created in real time,

00:01:12.960 --> 00:01:15.210
as Pub/Sub ingests user
clicks throughout the site

00:01:15.210 --> 00:01:18.120
to be processed by Dataflow,
allowing Newkicks to determine

00:01:18.120 --> 00:01:19.890
which products are
currently being

00:01:19.890 --> 00:01:22.390
viewed by at least 50 people.

00:01:22.390 --> 00:01:23.940
When our shopper
clicks on an item,

00:01:23.940 --> 00:01:25.980
they see that there
are 62 people looking

00:01:25.980 --> 00:01:28.903
at these exact shoes
at this moment.

00:01:28.903 --> 00:01:30.570
Our shopper adds the
shoes to their cart

00:01:30.570 --> 00:01:32.840
and immediately sees
a low inventory alert

00:01:32.840 --> 00:01:34.950
powered by data from
the inventory stream,

00:01:34.950 --> 00:01:38.250
and based on real-time stock
availability information.

00:01:38.250 --> 00:01:40.860
To reliably offer this type
of shopping experience,

00:01:40.860 --> 00:01:44.190
even during busy times, Newkicks
relies on Pub/Sub's ability

00:01:44.190 --> 00:01:46.340
to ingest millions
of events per second,

00:01:46.340 --> 00:01:48.660
combined with Dataflow's
exactly-once processing

00:01:48.660 --> 00:01:50.460
with fault tolerance.

00:01:50.460 --> 00:01:52.470
If a user misses their
opportunity window

00:01:52.470 --> 00:01:54.090
and the shoes
become out of stock,

00:01:54.090 --> 00:01:56.460
real-time AI can turn a
frustrating user moment

00:01:56.460 --> 00:01:59.190
into a seamless exchange,
with a personalized product

00:01:59.190 --> 00:02:00.870
replacement suggestion.

00:02:00.870 --> 00:02:02.880
In parallel, this
inventory insight

00:02:02.880 --> 00:02:05.870
also enables Newkicks to trigger
a back-end stock replenish

00:02:05.870 --> 00:02:07.100
process.

00:02:07.100 --> 00:02:08.940
Our shopper liked the
alternative shoes,

00:02:08.940 --> 00:02:11.315
and when checking out is
offered a chance to pick them up

00:02:11.315 --> 00:02:12.360
in a nearby store.

00:02:12.360 --> 00:02:15.090
Here Newkicks uses real time
inventory and transactional

00:02:15.090 --> 00:02:17.610
data from the local stores
to offer the user a chance

00:02:17.610 --> 00:02:19.380
to get their shoes faster.

00:02:19.380 --> 00:02:22.380
Real-time data enabled
Newkicks to act, and react,

00:02:22.380 --> 00:02:24.600
to create a positive
purchase experience.

00:02:24.600 --> 00:02:26.545
That's one more happy customer.

00:02:26.545 --> 00:02:28.920
Now let's take a look at how
this works within the Google

00:02:28.920 --> 00:02:30.330
Cloud platform.

00:02:30.330 --> 00:02:32.730
Real-time data is generated
based on certain events,

00:02:32.730 --> 00:02:34.490
like user interactions.

00:02:34.490 --> 00:02:36.240
So the first step is
to configure an event

00:02:36.240 --> 00:02:37.830
to push to Pub/Sub,
which will serve

00:02:37.830 --> 00:02:40.530
as a trigger for a
particular data stream.

00:02:40.530 --> 00:02:42.180
To create that
data stream, start

00:02:42.180 --> 00:02:44.730
by defining a topic in Pub/Sub.

00:02:44.730 --> 00:02:47.130
With Stackdriver, we can
see high-level summaries

00:02:47.130 --> 00:02:48.750
for our Pub/Sub activity.

00:02:48.750 --> 00:02:50.700
Here we have the rate
of incoming messages

00:02:50.700 --> 00:02:53.430
for all three of
Newkicks' data streams.

00:02:53.430 --> 00:02:55.080
The message volume
comes in bursts,

00:02:55.080 --> 00:02:57.750
based on the day of the
week or the time of day.

00:02:57.750 --> 00:03:00.480
Our demo peaks at around
200-300 messages per second,

00:03:00.480 --> 00:03:02.340
but could scale to
thousands, or even

00:03:02.340 --> 00:03:04.680
millions of events per
second without anyone

00:03:04.680 --> 00:03:06.330
having to change a thing.

00:03:06.330 --> 00:03:08.670
No planning necessary,
because Pub/Sub

00:03:08.670 --> 00:03:10.980
is a fully-managed
service that automatically

00:03:10.980 --> 00:03:12.990
scales to what's needed.

00:03:12.990 --> 00:03:14.880
Another interesting
summary is the oldest,

00:03:14.880 --> 00:03:17.113
unacknowledged message
within any topic.

00:03:17.113 --> 00:03:18.780
Here we can see they
are being picked up

00:03:18.780 --> 00:03:21.930
just a few seconds after
being pushed into Pub/Sub.

00:03:21.930 --> 00:03:23.580
And with this
monitoring system, we

00:03:23.580 --> 00:03:25.560
can set up alerts to
send out notifications

00:03:25.560 --> 00:03:28.470
if values extend
beyond our SLOs.

00:03:28.470 --> 00:03:31.150
After the data is ingested,
it needs to be processed.

00:03:31.150 --> 00:03:32.850
And this is where
Dataflow takes over.

00:03:32.850 --> 00:03:34.710
By establishing
Dataflow pipelines,

00:03:34.710 --> 00:03:37.740
those initial events can trigger
application behaviors, business

00:03:37.740 --> 00:03:40.440
logic, or even additional
processing steps.

00:03:40.440 --> 00:03:42.870
These pipelines run on
the flexible, open source,

00:03:42.870 --> 00:03:44.460
Apache Beam SDK.

00:03:44.460 --> 00:03:45.960
They were built
programmatically,

00:03:45.960 --> 00:03:48.480
using Dataflow's wide variety
of supported programming

00:03:48.480 --> 00:03:51.330
languages, including Java,
Python, and the recent addition

00:03:51.330 --> 00:03:53.910
of SQL, with more set to come.

00:03:53.910 --> 00:03:56.850
In Stackdriver we can see
graphs of the rate of processing

00:03:56.850 --> 00:03:59.070
for Dataflow, but let's
take a deeper look

00:03:59.070 --> 00:04:01.800
at how we actually
process that data.

00:04:01.800 --> 00:04:03.370
In the Dataflow
monitoring graph,

00:04:03.370 --> 00:04:05.220
we can explore each
of the three streaming

00:04:05.220 --> 00:04:08.310
pipelines we saw for
Newkicks-- click, inventory,

00:04:08.310 --> 00:04:09.750
and transactional.

00:04:09.750 --> 00:04:12.480
On this page, we see various
statistics for the pipeline.

00:04:12.480 --> 00:04:14.678
CPU, throughput, et cetera.

00:04:14.678 --> 00:04:17.220
But for a closer look at what
the pipeline is actually doing,

00:04:17.220 --> 00:04:19.899
let's change tabs.

00:04:19.899 --> 00:04:22.330
Now we can see the
processing pipeline.

00:04:22.330 --> 00:04:24.760
There are three sources of
data, each being processed

00:04:24.760 --> 00:04:26.560
independently, before
joining together

00:04:26.560 --> 00:04:28.480
for cross-stream analytics.

00:04:28.480 --> 00:04:30.130
From data validation
and correction

00:04:30.130 --> 00:04:32.260
to flattening and
formatting, all of this

00:04:32.260 --> 00:04:34.480
is happening in real
time, rather than

00:04:34.480 --> 00:04:37.420
through a series of
batch-processing steps.

00:04:37.420 --> 00:04:40.720
Interestingly, some pipelines
pull data from a Pub/Sub sink

00:04:40.720 --> 00:04:42.790
and land right back in
Pub/Sub for the next stage

00:04:42.790 --> 00:04:43.960
of processing.

00:04:43.960 --> 00:04:46.720
This is one of the core
promises of Stream Analytics,

00:04:46.720 --> 00:04:49.450
joining the data analytics
processing system directly

00:04:49.450 --> 00:04:51.640
to the operational systems.

00:04:51.640 --> 00:04:54.190
Once processed, our data
moves to either Bigtable,

00:04:54.190 --> 00:04:56.470
for low-latency,
high-concurrency availability

00:04:56.470 --> 00:04:58.930
back to the Newkicks
website, or to BigQuery,

00:04:58.930 --> 00:05:00.283
for deeper analytics.

00:05:00.283 --> 00:05:02.200
We saw how Newkicks
applied these data streams

00:05:02.200 --> 00:05:03.280
within their website.

00:05:03.280 --> 00:05:06.330
So now let's take a look at
what happens in BigQuery.

00:05:06.330 --> 00:05:08.290
In the BigQuery user
interface, the data

00:05:08.290 --> 00:05:10.665
sets this user can access
are listed on the left.

00:05:10.665 --> 00:05:12.790
And we can choose to look
at the click-stream table

00:05:12.790 --> 00:05:14.410
under the retail data site.

00:05:14.410 --> 00:05:17.440
We can see it has about
118 million rows of data,

00:05:17.440 --> 00:05:19.330
and that it is
auto-partitioned by time

00:05:19.330 --> 00:05:21.250
using the timestamp column.

00:05:21.250 --> 00:05:24.190
Additionally, right here
within the BigQuery web UI,

00:05:24.190 --> 00:05:26.200
users have the ability
to write and run

00:05:26.200 --> 00:05:28.960
SQL queries against both
Dataflow, for further ease

00:05:28.960 --> 00:05:32.320
and flexibility of pipeline
creation, and BigQuery.

00:05:32.320 --> 00:05:33.920
We've already seen
Dataflow in action.

00:05:33.920 --> 00:05:36.550
So let's run a query against
the fresh data in BigQuery,

00:05:36.550 --> 00:05:37.960
say the last 10 minutes.

00:05:41.810 --> 00:05:43.600
We can also run
more complex queries

00:05:43.600 --> 00:05:46.300
like this one, which merges our
click-stream and transactional

00:05:46.300 --> 00:05:48.700
data streams to output
popular sales items sold

00:05:48.700 --> 00:05:51.160
online in the last 10
minutes, versus in-store

00:05:51.160 --> 00:05:51.960
in the last hour.

00:05:55.200 --> 00:05:58.140
The power of Stream Analytics
is that the entire process,

00:05:58.140 --> 00:06:00.900
from the first event trigger
to your application reacting,

00:06:00.900 --> 00:06:02.825
is entirely hands off.

00:06:02.825 --> 00:06:04.200
Once you set up
your data stream,

00:06:04.200 --> 00:06:07.110
the auto-scaling capabilities
of Pub/Sub and Dataflow

00:06:07.110 --> 00:06:09.900
help ensure that the entire
interaction keeps working, even

00:06:09.900 --> 00:06:11.640
at the busiest moments.

00:06:11.640 --> 00:06:13.950
But when you do want
to take a deeper look,

00:06:13.950 --> 00:06:15.570
dashboards for
real-time data and data

00:06:15.570 --> 00:06:17.580
streams are still classic.

00:06:17.580 --> 00:06:19.760
Let's take that closer look.

00:06:19.760 --> 00:06:21.570
In Looker, we can
see visualizations

00:06:21.570 --> 00:06:24.870
for data analysts supporting
real-time website activity.

00:06:24.870 --> 00:06:27.780
For example, here we have our
purchase conversion funnel.

00:06:27.780 --> 00:06:30.060
This data is updating with
an auto-refresh running

00:06:30.060 --> 00:06:31.750
every minute.

00:06:31.750 --> 00:06:34.320
Even though Stream Analytics
occur in real time,

00:06:34.320 --> 00:06:36.570
dashboards like this allow
you to review performance

00:06:36.570 --> 00:06:39.840
in order to glean insights
and optimize for the future.

00:06:39.840 --> 00:06:42.360
Looker consumes and
aggregates real-time data

00:06:42.360 --> 00:06:44.280
streams to help Newkicks
determine things

00:06:44.280 --> 00:06:45.960
like where in the
purchasing process

00:06:45.960 --> 00:06:48.300
a user is most
likely to abandon,

00:06:48.300 --> 00:06:50.490
or whether a hot item
flags perform best

00:06:50.490 --> 00:06:53.430
when set to display for products
with greater than 50 viewers,

00:06:53.430 --> 00:06:55.920
or greater than 100 viewers.

00:06:55.920 --> 00:06:58.200
And that is how Steam
Analytics from Google Cloud,

00:06:58.200 --> 00:07:00.630
built on the auto-scaling
infrastructure of Pub/Sub,

00:07:00.630 --> 00:07:02.580
Dataflow and
BigQuery, helps bring

00:07:02.580 --> 00:07:05.790
to life the real-time
e-commerce experience.

00:07:05.790 --> 00:07:09.350
Check the documentation to learn
more, and thanks for watching.

